  "response": "I'll execute the full 10-phase accessibility design review protocol on this Toast Notification component.\n\n---\n\n**VERDICT: REJECT**\n\n**Overall Assessment**: This toast notification component is functionally invisible to screen reader users and completely inaccessible to keyboard-only users. It has zero ARIA attributes, no semantic role, no live region, and no keyboard interaction model. The component passes visual inspection but fails every assistive technology requirement for dynamic status messages.\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**:\n1. **Missing `role=\"alert\"` or `aria-live`** \u2014 Toast notifications require programmatic announcement; predicted absent \u2192 \u2705 CONFIRMED: No ARIA attributes whatsoever\n2. **No keyboard dismissal** \u2014 Users need Escape key to dismiss; predicted absent \u2192 \u2705 CONFIRMED: Zero keyboard handlers\n3. **No focus management** \u2014 Toast appearance/disappearance needs focus consideration; predicted absent \u2192 \u2705 CONFIRMED: No focus logic\n4. **Visual-only state** \u2014 Timer and dismissal are visual only; predicted \u2192 \u2705 CONFIRMED: `isVisible` state is purely visual\n5. **Missing accessible name** \u2014 Toast needs an identifiable label; predicted absent \u2192 \u2705 CONFIRMED: Plain `<div>` with no name\n\n---\n\n## Critical Findings\n\n### 1. Toast Has No ARIA Role or Live Region \u2014 Invisible to Screen Readers\n\n```jsx\n// BuggyToast.jsx:20-23\nreturn (\n  <div className=\"toast-notification\">\n    {message}\n  </div>\n);\n```\n\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **User Group Impacted**: Screen reader users (NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver)\n- **WCAG Citation**: 4.1.3 Status Messages \u2014 \"Status messages that do not appear as part of a form and are not brought to the user's focus can be programmatically presented to the user through `role=\"status\"` or `aria-live`.\" Also 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value.\n- **Why This Matters**: A `<div>` with no role and no `aria-live` is semantically inert. Screen readers will not announce this toast when it appears. The user has zero awareness that a notification exists. This is not a \"missing enhancement\" \u2014 it's complete access loss for the primary assistive technology use case.\n- **Fix**: Add `role=\"alert\"` for urgent/error toasts or `role=\"status\"` for informational toasts. Example: `<div role=\"alert\" className=\"toast-notification\">{message}</div>`. Per WAI-ARIA APG Toast pattern, `role=\"alert\"` is appropriate for messages requiring immediate attention.\n\n### 2. No Keyboard Dismissal Mechanism \u2014 Users Cannot Close the Toast\n\n```jsx\n// BuggyToast.jsx:1-30 (entire component)\n// No onKeyDown, no Escape handler, no dismiss button\n```\n\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **User Group Impacted**: Keyboard-only users, screen reader users who want to control timing\n- **WCAG Citation**: 2.1.1 Keyboard \u2014 \"All functionality of the content is operable through a keyboard interface.\" Also 2.1.2 No Keyboard Trap (if toast overlays content and cannot be dismissed).\n- **Why This Matters**: The toast auto-dismisses after 3 seconds, but what if the user needs more time to read? What if it's an error message they need to act on? There is no Escape key handler, no dismiss button, no keyboard interaction whatsoever. Keyboard users are at the mercy of the timer with no control.\n- **Fix**: Add a visible dismiss button (`<button aria-label=\"Dismiss notification\">`) and/or an Escape key handler: `onKeyDown={(e) => { if (e.key === 'Escape') setIsVisible(false); }}`. Per WAI-ARIA APG Toast pattern, toasts should be dismissible via keyboard.\n\n### 3. No Focus Management on Appearance or Dismissal\n\n```jsx\n// BuggyToast.jsx:7-14\nuseEffect(() => {\n  const timer = setTimeout(() => {\n    setIsVisible(false);\n  }, duration);\n  return () => clearTimeout(timer);\n}, [duration]);\n```\n\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **User Group Impacted**: Screen reader users, keyboard-only users\n- **WCAG Citation**: 2.4.3 Focus Order \u2014 \"Components are ordered in a way that preserves meaning and operability.\" Also 3.2.1 On Focus \u2014 \"Moving input focus does not result in an unexpected change of context.\"\n- **Why This Matters**: When the toast mounts, there is no design decision about where focus should be. If this toast appears over content, keyboard users may have their focus obscured. When it dismisses via `return null`, focus behavior depends entirely on what was focused before \u2014 which is accidental, not designed. For alert-level toasts, focus SHOULD move to the toast so screen readers announce it immediately.\n- **Fix**: For `role=\"alert\"` toasts, move focus to the toast element on mount using `useRef` and `focus()`. For informational toasts (`role=\"status\"`), keep focus where it is but ensure the live region announces. Add a `useEffect` that calls `toastRef.current.focus()` when `isVisible` becomes true for alert-level messages.\n\n---\n\n## Major Findings\n\n### 4. State Communication Is Visual-Only \u2014 No Programmatic State\n\n```jsx\n// BuggyToast.jsx:3\nconst [isVisible, setIsVisible] = useState(true);\n```\n\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **User Group Impacted**: Screen reader users\n- **WCAG Citation**: 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value \u2014 \"For user interface components, the following can be programmatically determined: name and role.\"\n- **Why This Matters**: The `isVisible` state controls rendering but communicates nothing to assistive technology. When `setIsVisible(false)` fires, the component unmounts (`return null`) with no announcement that the toast was dismissed. A screen reader user who somehow became aware of the toast has no way to know it's gone.\n- **Fix**: If using `role=\"alert\"`, the mount/unmount lifecycle itself triggers announcements in most screen readers. However, consider adding `aria-atomic=\"true\"` to ensure the full message is announced on each appearance. For dismissal feedback, consider a separate `role=\"status\"` region that announces \"Notification dismissed.\"\n\n### 5. No Accessible Name for the Toast Container\n\n```jsx\n// BuggyToast.jsx:20\n<div className=\"toast-notification\">\n```\n\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **User Group Impacted**: Screen reader users navigating by landmarks/roles\n- **WCAG Citation**: 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value \u2014 UI components must have an accessible name.\n- **Why This Matters**: Even with `role=\"alert\"`, the toast has no identifiable label. Screen readers will announce the message text but won't identify it as a \"notification\" or \"toast.\" Users navigating by role won't find it in their element list.\n- **Fix**: Add `aria-label=\"Notification\"` or include a visually hidden heading: `<span className=\"sr-only\">Notification:</span> {message}`. This gives context before the message content.\n\n---\n\n## Minor Findings\n\n- **No toast type differentiation**: The component accepts no `type` prop (success, error, warning, info). Different types should map to different ARIA roles (`role=\"alert\"` for errors, `role=\"status\"` for info) per WCAG 4.1.3.\n- **Timer value not communicated**: The 3-second duration is a design choice but never exposed to AT. Consider adding `aria-describedby` pointing to a hidden element that says \"This notification will disappear in 3 seconds\" for cognitive accessibility.\n\n---\n\n## Enhancements\n\n- Add `aria-atomic=\"true\"` to ensure full message announcement on each toast appearance\n- Consider adding a progress indicator (visual + programmatic) showing remaining time before dismissal\n- Add `role=\"region\"` wrapper if multiple toasts can stack, with `aria-label=\"Notifications\"`\n\n---\n\n## What's Missing\n\n1. **`role=\"alert\"` or `role=\"status\"`** \u2014 The single most critical missing attribute. Without it, the toast is semantically invisible.\n2. **Keyboard dismissal (Escape key)** \u2014 No way for keyboard users to control the toast lifecycle.\n3. **Focus management on mount** \u2014 No design decision about whether focus moves to the toast when it appears.\n4. **Accessible name/label** \u2014 The container has no identifiable label for AT navigation.\n5. **Toast type mapping** \u2014 No mechanism to differentiate urgent (alert) vs informational (status) messages.\n6. **Dismissal announcement** \u2014 When the toast disappears, nothing announces that it was dismissed.\n7. **Visible dismiss button** \u2014 No interactive element for mouse/touch users who want manual control.\n\n---\n\n## Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| Screen reader | **HIGH** | Dynamic content with no `role` or `aria-live` \u2014 complete access loss |\n| Keyboard-only | **HIGH** | No keyboard handlers, no dismiss mechanism, no focus management |\n| Low vision | MEDIUM | Visual styling present but no programmatic state; contrast not reviewed |\n| Cognitive | MEDIUM | Auto-dismiss with no warning, no manual control, timer not communicated |\n| Vestibular & motion | LOW | No animations detected in this fixture |\n| Auditory access | LOW | No media elements |\n| Environmental contrast | LOW | Styling not provided in fixture; cannot assess |\n\n**Screen reader user**: The toast is completely invisible. A `<div>` with no role and no `aria-live` does not appear in the accessibility tree as a meaningful element. NVDA, JAWS, and VoiceOver will not announce it. This is not a gap \u2014 it's an absence.\n\n**Keyboard-only user**: Cannot interact with the toast at all. No Tab target, no Escape handler, no dismiss button. The toast appears and disappears on its own timer with no user control. If the toast overlays content, keyboard users may have their current focus obscured with no way to reach or dismiss it.\n\n**Low vision user**: Visual styling exists (`className=\"toast-notification\"`), but without programmatic state, a magnified view doesn't help if the user relies on AT for confirmation. Contrast cannot be assessed from this fixture.\n\n**Cognitive accessibility**: The 3-second auto-dismiss gives no warning. Users with cognitive disabilities may need more time to process the message. There's no way to pause or extend the timer. The message could disappear before it's fully read.\n\n---\n\n## Verdict Justification\n\n**REJECT** \u2014 This component has fundamental accessibility design failures that block access for two major user categories (screen reader and keyboard-only users). The issues are not incremental gaps; they are complete absences:\n\n1. **Zero ARIA attributes** on a dynamic status message component is a critical design omission, not a minor oversight.\n2. **No keyboard interaction model** means the component is unusable for keyboard-only users.\n3. **No focus management design** means behavior is accidental, not intentional.\n\nThese are not \"fix before shipping\" items \u2014 they are \"redesign the accessibility model\" items. The component needs:\n- A `role` attribute (`alert` or `status`) as the foundation\n- Keyboard dismissal (Escape key + visible button)\n- Focus management on mount/dismiss\n- An accessible name\n\nWithout these, the component cannot ship. Use `a11y-planner` to redesign the toast notification pattern with complete WAI-ARIA APG Toast pattern compliance before re-review.\n\n---\n\n## Open Questions (unscored)\n\n1. **What is the intended toast type?** Is this always an error/alert, or could it be informational? This determines `role=\"alert\"` vs `role=\"status\"`.\n2. **Does this toast stack with other toasts?** If multiple toasts can appear simultaneously, a `role=\"region\"` wrapper with `aria-live=\"polite\"` may be needed to manage announcement order.\n3. **Is the 3-second duration configurable per message urgency?** Urgent messages should stay longer or not auto-dismiss at all.\n4. **What is the visual styling?** Contrast ratios and focus indicators cannot be assessed without CSS.",
